<HTML> 
<BODY BGCOLOR="#000000" TEXT="#FFFFFF" LINK="#90FFFF" VLINK="#CCCCCC">
<FONT FACE="Arial,Helvetica">
<H3>
<CENTER>
<FONT COLOR="#FFFF00">
Modifications Possible for FIAT Tipo 16V</FONT>
</CENTER></H3>

These are the modifications I have made to my 16V Tipo, whether they made any
difference, whether they were worth it and whether they caused any side effects.

<UL>
<LI><A HREF="#filter">K &amp; N 57i Induction Kit</A>
<LI><A HREF="#sparkplugs">Splitfire Spark Plugs</A>
<LI><A HREF="#discs">Tarox 12 Groove Discs</A>
<LI><A HREF="#exhaust">Supersprint Exhaust</A>
<LI><A HREF="#chip">Superchip</A>
<LI><A HREF="#springs">Spax Uprated Lowered Springs</A>
<LI><A HREF="#knob">Momo Race Airleather Aluminum Gear Knob</A>
<LI><A HREF="#wheels">TSW Evo R wheels</A>
<LI><A HREF="#radar">RadioShack X&middot;K&middot;Ka Superwide Radar Dectector</A>
<LI><A HREF="#steering_wheel">Momo Race Steering Wheel</A>
</UL>
<HR>
<P>
<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="filter">K &amp; N 57i Induction Kit</A></FONT></H3>
<IMG SRC="kn.jpg" ALIGN=left WIDTH=313 HEIGHT=78 ALT="K &amp; N Air Filter">
        I had originally tried a JETEX direct replacement air filter but noticed no increase
        in performance, although not having to clean it or replace it every service was a
        distinct advantage. Knowing the engines performance would improve if it could breath
        better I ordered the <A HREF="http://www.knfilters.com" target="_blank">K&amp;N</A> air filter kit costing &pound;74.90

<BR><BR><IMG SRC="airfilter.jpg" ALIGN=right WIDTH=123 HEIGHT=115 ALT="57i Induction kit">

        Fitting of the filter is very easy. 
        Unbolt the plastic cold air feed from above the radiator.
        Disconnect it from
        the flexihose that joins it to the box containing the air filter and
        then remove this flexihose.
        Unscrew the air filter box from it's mounting points. Undo the
        jubilee clip holding the flexihose to the inlet manifold. Depending
        on the year of the car there will be either one or two small hoses
        attached to this flexible hose and undo these and the whole of the
        original inlet trunking can be removed leaving just the air flow
        meter attached to the inlet manifold.
<P>
        The K&amp;N filter supplied was a large cone design with hose diameter of some 80mm
        that connected via a short steel tube to the air flow meter
        using a couple of huge jubilee clips. On pre 1993 16V TIPO's
        only a single hose joins the steel tube, on post 1993 cars a cold start value hose and
        a breather hose connect to it. Mine was the first post 1993 car they had supplied so the
        steel tube only had attachments for a single hose. I spoke to and faxed K&amp;N detailing
        the placement of the second hose needed. K&amp;N's customer support is first class,
        within a day I received a new steel tube with two hose attachment points and
        a specially shaped second hose which fitted perfectly.
<P>
        To finish off the installation a flexible large diameter metal hose is supplied that
        directs cold air from the front of the car over the air filter. This required part of
        the front grill to be cut away. This sounds worse than it really is as close to the
        near side front fog light is a perforated panel ready to be cut out.
        The only problem with the whole set up is trying to get enough cold air to
        the air filter, the aluminized flexihose supplied is a good start but
        it needs a more effective solution.
<P>
        As for performance, a noticeable increase in power was felt. K&amp;N themselves say that
        they noticed an increase of 8 bhp. Driving at a constant speed or accelerating gently
        only a small increase of noise was noticed. I was always aware of the less than subtle
        exhaust note of the car but floor the throttle now and the induction
        roar is terrific. It completely drowns out the exhaust note with a gorgeous deep
        growl which changes to a higher pitch at about 4500 rpm. It sounds more like
        an American stock car now (almost like it has twin webbers).
        When the engine is switched off a loud final suck is heard.
<P>
        Please make sure you feed the filter with plenty of cold air.
	If inadequate cold air is directed at the filter the engine will
	instead suck warm air from around the engine. Warm air
	is less dense than cold air and the engine will actually perform
	worse. In some circumstances the engine has been known to
	'pink'. It might be worth fitting an extension pipe to the filter
	such that it sits where the old air box used to be. Read how
        <A HREF="../owners/alexis_grant.html">Alexis Grant</A> solved this problem.

<P>
        An alternative would be to replace the original
        air filter with a direct replacement K&amp;N filter. Keep the original
        trunking between the engine and air box but use a large diameter pipe
        to feed cold air from the front of the car to the front of the air box. This provides a ram-air
        effect, increasing engine power the faster the car is travelling. No noticeable
        increase in noise is heard as most of the trunking is left intact, however
        an increase in power of 8 bhp is noticed.
</P>
<HR>
<P>
<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="sparkplugs">Splitfire Spark Plugs</A></FONT></H3>
<IMG SRC="splitfire.jpg" ALIGN=left WIDTH=119 HEIGHT=111 ALT="Splitfire Spark Plug">

        I installed <A HREF="http://www.splitfire.com" target="_blank">Splitfire</A> spark plugs costing &pound;27.95 when the standard FIAT ones were
        due for renewal.
        I really wanted the Bosch Super 4 at &pound;17.99 as they represented excellent value for
        money with similar claimed performance. I can't honestly say that I noticed any difference
        in performance between the Splitfire plugs and standard FIAT plugs. The
        design of the ground electrode seems a good idea though and can only help expose
        the spark to the air/fuel mixture.
        It's only really worth changing to them when the standard ones are due for renewal, don't
        just replace them for the sake of it.
</P>
<HR>
<P>
<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="discs">Tarox 12 Groove Discs</A></FONT></H3>
<IMG SRC="tarox.jpg" ALIGN=left WIDTH=215 HEIGHT=44 ALT="Tarox logo">

        The standard FIAT discs and pads lasted 58,000 miles before the lips on
        the discs became too pronounced to pass an MOT. I'm not particularly heavy
        on the brakes, but even so this life span is pretty impressive.

        I have always been happy with the brakes on the 16V TIPO, the 285mm
        diameter discs and powerful servo work very well. However, now that they
        were due for renewal I thought I would see what upgrades were available.

        What I really wanted to	do was fit the slightly larger discs and twin
        pot
        <A HREF="http://www.brembo.it" target="_blank">Brembo</A> callipers from the FIAT Coupe Turbo. This however would require
        fitting 16&quot; wheels and changing the brake servo in order to provide the extra
        fluid needed. To put me off completely the Brembo callipers
        cost about &pound;250 pounds each.
        Standard FIAT replacement discs are about &pound;140 and pads &pound;90 so I decided
        to ask around to see what alternatives there were. The conclusion was that
        there are not that many and they are all very expensive.
<BR>
<BR>
        <IMG SRC="discs.jpg" ALIGN=right WIDTH=322 HEIGHT=224 ALT="Tarox 12 Groove Discs &amp; Pads">
        I eventually decided on
        <A HREF="http://www.tarox.com" target="_blank">Tarox</A> 12 groove discs and matching pads. These
        should hopefully give the pads a bit more of an edge to bite against, dispense
        the heat more efficiently under heavy braking and stop the pads from glazing over.

        The best quote I got for a pair of discs and set of pads was &pound;250 from
        <A HREF="aldon.html">Aldon Automotive Ltd</A>.
        As always it is best to phone around since the highest quote I got was
        &pound;366.
<BR>
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
        Fitting the discs was easy enough as they are a direct replacement. The
        only difficult part is opening the callipers wide enough to fit the new
        pads. Even with the brake hose disconnected the pressure required to
        move them was enormous.

        One thing I'm not very happy about but is probably going to be the case for all
        aftermarket pads is that only one pair had a wear indicator. Original sets of
        FIAT pads have a wear indicator for each pair which is useful as one pair
        may wear down quicker than the other. Wanting to keep the car working fully
        I thought I would ask Tarox to send me another pad complete with wear indicator.
        I spoke to the main Tarox distributor
        in the UK and they were extremely unhelpful. I have never received such
        poor customer support and never wish to again. They refused to send me another
        pad, they would not ask Tarox in Italy to send one, they would not split one
        of their sets in stock, their best solution was to
        buy another set of pads and throw three of them away. I was dumbfounded and
        wrote to Tarox in Italy but never received a reply.

        Performance of the discs is very good, the problem is you tend to find
        yourself braking later and harder as you gain more confidence in the
        power of your brakes, forgetting that not everybody can stop equally
        as well!

