</P>
<HR>
<P>
<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="exhaust">Supersprint Exhaust</A></FONT></H3>
<IMG SRC="supersprint.jpg" ALIGN=left WIDTH=300 HEIGHT=52 ALT="SuperSprint Logo">


        My original exhaust system, shown in the diagram below, failed the cars first MOT at 58,000 miles. The emissions of CO and HC were well
        within the limits however the Lamda figure was wrong.
        This was happening because the tail pipe had rusted 75% of the way around where
        it joined the rear silencer. This allowed air to be sucked into the exhaust gases

<IMG SRC="original.jpg" ALIGN=right WIDTH=375 HEIGHT=196 ALT="Original FIAT Exhaust System">
        causing the test equipment's lamda sensor to think the engine was running lean.
        Given that the exhaust had to be replaced I thought it was time to try a performance version. I tried
        <A HREF="http://www.borla.com" target="_blank">Borla</A>,
        <A HREF="http://www.sebring.de" target="_blank">Sebring</A>,
        <A HREF="http://www.scorpion-exhausts.com" target="_blank">Scorpion</A>,
        <A HREF="http://www.janspeed.co.uk" target="_blank">Janspeed</A>,
        <A HREF="http://www.magnaflow.com" target="_blank">Magnaflow</A>,
        <A HREF="http://www.peco.co.uk" target="_blank">Peco</A>,
        Magnex, Jetex and
        Mongoose
        but none sold exhausts for the Tipo 16V. The only
        companies that did were
        <A HREF="http://www.bosal.be" target="_blank">Bosal</A>,
        ANSA and
        <A HREF="http://www.supersprint.com" target="_blank">Supersprint</A>.
<P>

        I opted for the Supersprint exhaust, yes it was
        very expensive (~&pound;500) but the performance promised, the impeccable build quality and finish
        swung it for me. The tubing is still only 2&quot;
        diameter which is a bit on the narrow side but at least it means that it mates directly to the
        existing manifold and catalytic
        converter (I larger diameter tube of 2 1/2&quot; would I understand only push the torque curve
        further up the rev range). The tubing and absorption material is stainless steel but the outer
        silencer shields are aluminised steel as they are less prone to cracking and produce a deeper
        less tinny sound than stainless steel would.
<P>
        Details of other exhaust systems can be found in the
        <A HREF="../faq/faq.html#alternative_exhausts">FAQ</A> section.

<P>

<IMG SRC="cat.jpg" ALIGN=right WIDTH=332 HEIGHT=91 ALT="Catalytic Converter Replacement">

        I have replaced the catalytic converter with the straight through pipe
        but is was not as simple as first hoped.
        FIAT, in their infinite wisdom had attached the lamda sensor to the top
        of the catalytic converter rather than to the exhaust manifold.
        There is a place on the exhaust manifold where it could be mounted
        horizontally but this would be very prone to damage.
        A lead is molded to the
        top of the lamda sensor and the other end is attached to another lead at the
        rear of the engine bay next to the MAP sensor behind the inlet
        manifold. To avoid any unnecessary twisting of the
        lead the exhaust, once unbolted, has to be rotated around
        the sensor. This is quite a performance so make sure you take
        the car to an understanding garage.
        As you can see from the image above it is not completely a straight
        through pipe as it does contain a small sound absorbing section. I
        don't think this is absolutely necessary as the exhaust is not particularly
        loud. Maybe you can find an alternative without this.
<P>
        Please read this
        <A HREF="../faq/faq.html#catalytic_converter">FAQ</A>
        before considering replacing your catalytic converter.
<P>
<IMG SRC="complete.jpg" ALIGN=left WIDTH=168 HEIGHT=385 ALT="Complete Exhaust System">

        The picture on the left shows the complete exhaust system.
        The yellow lines indicate the joins between the catalytic converter
        replacement and the center section and between the center section
        and the rear box. Unfortunately the parts are not shown to scale due to
        the perspective nature of the photograph.

        The placement of the centre silencer is very different from the
        standard exhaust in order to give a straighter flow for the gases. This silencer now fits close
        behind the catalytic converter requiring the heat shielding to be reshaped to cater for the
        wider exhaust at this point. A new bracket is supplied with the exhaust but no instructions
        as to where or how it should be fitted. This took some 15 minutes to work out!!

<P><IMG SRC="tailpipe.jpg" ALIGN=right WIDTH=183 HEIGHT=214 ALT="Tailpipe">

        Don't be alarmed when you see that the rear silencer has a huge dent in the side. This is intentional
        and is designed to avoid fouling the rear suspension. The tailpipe is also a very snug
        (but perfect) fit in the rear valance with only about 5mm clearance. The tailpipe is polished and some
        90mm in diameter which sounds large but looks just right on the car. It slopes slightly upward which
        looks good but I imagine it might cause problems if water is allowed to stand there for too long.
<P>
        To reshape the heat shielding and fit the extra bracket will take about 45 minutes. To replace
        the catalytic converter takes a further 30 minutes so it is probably best to book the work
        into an exhaust specialist rather than just turning up. Expect to pay about &pound;30 pounds to
        have it fitted.
<P>
        As always it is best to phone at least ten retailers to get a range of prices as when you're paying
        this much a few percent can make a reasonable difference. I got prices between &pound;477 and &pound;563, guess
        which one I went for!! Due to the high cost of the Supersprint
        exhaust I recommend that you wait until the original exhaust has
        failed before replacing it unless (1) you have loads
        of money or (2) some serious engine tuning has been undertaken and
        is being compromised by the very restrictive nature of the original
        exhaust.
<P>

<IMG SRC="rear.jpg" ALIGN=right WIDTH=156 HEIGHT=203 ALT="Supersprint Exhaust Fitted">

        The performance increase was very noticeable. Supersprint say that you should get about 8 bhp extra
        from replacing the centre and rear silencers and a further 5 bhp if the
        catalytic converter is also replaced giving a grand total of 13 bhp.
        I would say this felt about right. The exhaust
        combined with the less restrictive air filter really allows the engine to breath well. The exhaust
        note is fantastic, lovely deep rumble that complements the induction roar of the air filter
        perfectly to give a more rounded sound. It's not ridiculously loud but don't floor the throttle
        unless you want to get noticed!!
<P>
        The original exhaust is a single piece affair from the catalytic
        converter back. This means its not possible to simply replace
        the original rear silencer with the Supersprint part. Ah ha you say,
        couldn't I just cut the original exhaust immediately before the rear silencer and
        join the Supersprint to that. I'm afraid not as the route taken by the
        Supersprint piping near the rear axle is very different from the original to improve the
        gas flow.
<P>

