<HR>
<P>
<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="chip">Superchip</A></FONT></H3>
<IMG SRC="chip.jpg" ALIGN=left WIDTH=127 HEIGHT=109 ALT="Chip">

        Chipping has to be one of easiest, cheapest and most common
        ways of	trying to get more power out of an engine. Apart from
        specialist chips there are two main UK manufactorers,
        <A HREF="http://www.superchips.co.uk" target="_blank">SuperChips</A>
        and 
        <A HREF="http://teamevans.co.nz/~eifs/STARCHIP.HTML" target="_blank">StarChip</A>.
        I sent off for information about both and opted for the SuperChip. Read my
	views about
        <A HREF="../faq/faq.html#chipping">chipping</A>.
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<HR>
<P>

<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="springs">Spax Uprated Lowered Springs</A></FONT></H3>
<A HREF="http://www.spax.co.uk" target="_blank">
<IMG SRC="spax.jpg" ALIGN=left BORDER=0 WIDTH=175 HEIGHT=109 ALT="Spax Logo"></A>
<IMG SRC="springs.jpg" ALIGN=right WIDTH=156 HEIGHT=134 ALT="Spax Sportsprings">
        I fitted a set of
        <A HREF="http://www.spax.co.uk" target="_blank"> Spax</A> lowered and uprated springs in October 1996 after
        the car had done some 46,000 miles. These cost &pound;85.42 and lowered
        the car by 35mm.

        The main reason for fitting the springs was to improve the cars
        handling. The car is now noticeably stiffer and rolls less
        when cornering. The ride is harsher but still tolerable, just don't
        expect any passengers to fall asleep.

        Lowering the car improved it's looks considerably. The gap between
        the front wheels and their arches is now uniform, rather than having a
        larger gap at the top. The rear arches are better but not perfect
        due to their slightly oval rather than circular shape. You should
        hopefully be able to see the difference in the pictures below.

<BR><BR>
<B>Things to check after the car is lowered:</B>
<UL>
<LI>
        <B>Suspension Geometry</B><BR>
        The suspension geometry must be checked after the car is lowered. The
        camber, caster and toe in can all be effected depending on the
        suspension system used. If not corrected these can cause tyre
        excessive tyre wear. The toe in can be checked very simply at
        most garages and is the most easily adjusted. The Tipo 16v should
        have a toe in of exactly 0.0<SUP>o</SUP>. On my Tipo, lowering it
        by 35mm resulted in a 0.5<SUP>o</SUP> toe out which is within the
        tolerance allowed but I had it corrected all the same. When the
        toe in is adjusted make sure that it is adjusted evenly on both sides
        to ensure that the steering wheel remains centered. If this is not
        done then take the car back and get the garage to adjust it again.
        The reason I say this is because the nut holding
        the steering wheel on is very tight making it difficult to remove the
        wheel. Should you remove the wheel you will also find it
        does not adjust in small enough angles to center correctly.
<BR><BR><LI>
        <B>Headlight Alignment</B><BR>
        What you must realize when you lower the
        car is that the headlights are also lowered. They therefore aim
        lower and hence their beams don't extend so far into the distance.
        This can be corrected using the headlight adjustment screws
        attached to the headlights. By lowering my car 35mm the adjustments
        are at now at their limits, just about able to raise the beam
        enought to pass an MOT. I would like to lower the car 
        by another 15mm - 20mm but it would probably fail the MOT.
<BR><BR><LI>
        <B>Shock Absorber Travel</B><BR>
        If the car is only lowered by 35mm the standard shock absorbers
        will be okay. Any lower though and you will need to buy shorter
        and uprated replacement items. If money is not a problem then it is
        obviously best to get a set of shock absorbers matched to the
        springs.
<BR><BR><LI>
        <B>Ground Clearance</B><BR>
        The problem with lowering a car, even by only 35mm is of course
        ground clearance. Along roads there is no real problem, however
        you can't simply drive off a curb as the side skirts are lower
        than the height of the curb. The standard exhaust will present no
        problems, however with the Supersprint exhaust fitted the center
        section is designed
        differently from the standard system having the silencer behind the
        catalytic converter rather than tucked up under the wheels. This
        results in it sitting slightly lower than standard system and has
        a tendency to ground even though the rest of the car appears to have
        cleared successfully.
</UL>

<TABLE BORDER="0" CELLPADDING="0" CELLSPACING="0" WIDTH="309" HEIGHT="128" ALIGN="RIGHT">
<TR>
        <TD>
        <IMG SRC="standard_springs.jpg" WIDTH=309 HEIGHT=128 ALT="Standard FIAT Springs">
        <IMG SRC="lowered_springs.jpg" WIDTH=309 HEIGHT=128 ALT="Spax 35mm Lowering Springs">
        </TD>
</TR></TABLE>

        The two biggest problems I have found with lowering the car are
        transporting it to garages and then leaving it in the
        hands of mechanics inexperienced in handling lowered cars.
        The font bumper is so low that should the car need to be
        transported on a trailer, the angle
        of the ramps needs to be quite shallow. This is not always
        easy to achieve. Even driving the car onto
        ramps in a garage requires extra car. If done too
        quickly the side skirts will catch as the car is half way onto the
        ramp. The car then does a good impression of a see-saw.
<BR><BR>
        Once at the garage the real fun begins. Most trolley jacks will
        not clear the side skirts. I once took the car to a FIAT garage
        for a service and it came back with deep blue gashes down the side
        skirts where they had rammed the jack underneath. On closer
        inspection the jacking point covers had been snapped off, breaking
        off the lugs and thus requiring glue to hold them on. I was not
        impressed and got the garage to order me a new pair of side skirts,
        need less to say I fitted them myself. Ever since I have removed the
        jacking point covers as a precaution before taking the car to
        any garage.
<BR><BR>
        On another occasion I went to pick the car up from a garage and
        noticed that the exhaust was sticking out from the rear at a funny
        angle. We raised the car on the jack and noticed that the flexible
        hose section of the exhaust had been stretched to it's limit and
        the braiding surrounding it was all shredded. The picture was
        completed by a square
        dent in the front of the center silencer. It turned out that one
        of the mechanics had driven the car onto the ramps and caught the
        underneath of the car on the solid metal block used for jacking
        up the front or rear suspension. They admitted
        everything and supplied and fitted a replacement flexible section
        which cost an amazing &pound;150 from FIAT.
<BR><BR>
        Getting the tyre valves or tyres replaced is not even straight
        forward. Once again the trolley jacks at the garage wouldn't fit
        under the car.
        It can be quite harrowing to see the mechanics trying to get the
        jack under the car. I have even seen them try and jack it up
        using the bumper or the lower suspension arms. It is very difficult
        to get them to admit defeat, the best thing to do is simply to get
        the standard car jack out and jack the car up before they cause
        any damage.
<BR><BR>
        Despite the above I am <B><U>very unhappy</U></B> with the quality of
        both the
        springs and the customer support given by Spax. Within six months
        of fitting the springs they had rusted causing the outer epoxy
        coating to split and fall off. The standard FIAT springs had been
        on the car for two years and were in mint condition when they were
        removed. I complained to Spax but they said it was only a cosmetic
        problem and weren't interested. I offered to drive the car to their
        headquarters so they could see for themselves but they said they
        would need the springs removed to run some tests on. This is a joke
        as I would have to take the car to a garage, have the springs
        removed and the original ones refitted. The reverse would be
        necessary when I got the springs back. For what this would cost
        I might as well buy a new set of springs - and you can guarantee
        that they won't be Spax this time.
        <B><U>I therefore can't recommend fitting 
        Spax springs</U></B> and when I replace
        the shock absorbers I will probably fit a set of high quality
        springs	such as
        <A HREF="http://www.eibach.com" target="_blank">Eibach</A>
        or
        <A HREF="http://www.koni.com" target="_blank">Koni</A>.

</P>

<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="knob">Momo Race Airleather Aluminum Gear Knob</A></FONT></H3>
<A HREF="http://www.momo.it" target="_blank">
<IMG SRC="momo.gif" ALIGN=left BORDER=0 WIDTH=49 HEIGHT=51 ALT="Momo Logo"></A>

        Purely for cosmetic reasons I have fitted a Momo replacement gear knob.
        The standard FIAT gear knob fitted to the car was
        was leather and had a good shape. However it was
        looking rather tired and dated so my
        <A HREF="../../eva/eva.html">Wife</A> brought
        me the Momo item. The gear knob cost &pound;45 and is code number 659169.
        <P>

        <IMG SRC="airleather.gif" ALIGN=right BORDER=0 WIDTH=263 HEIGHT=300 ALT="Race Airleather Aluminum"></A>

        The &quot;Race Airleather Aluminum&quot;
        gear knob is spherical with the lower
        half aluminum and the upper half perforated leather. On the top is
        an aluminum disc with 6 small holes and a Momo logo in the center.
        From the top it looks like the center of one of their steering
        wheels.
        <P>

        It is important to distinquish between
        the &quot;Race Airleather Aluminum&quot; and the
        &quot;Race Airleather&quot;. The 
        &quot;Race Airleather Aluminum&quot; is half leather and half
        aluminum where as the &quot;Race Airleather&quot; is leather
        top and bottom and does not look so good.
        <P>

        I will describe fitting the gear knob to a 3 door Tipo as I understand
        the earlier 5 door gear knob is fitted differently.

        <P>
   <OL>
        <LI>
        Remove the old gear knob boot. This is held to the center
        console by four plastic clips, one in the center of each side on the
        boot. As ever with these type of plastic clips, be careful not to snap
        them. The clips are attached to the bottom of a plastic ring that fits
        around the bottom of the boot. Inserting a large flat nose screwdriver
        horizontally between the console and the boot does the trick. Use two
        screwdrivers, one from the side and one from the bottom.

        <LI>
        Once the boot as been released the gear knob can be pulled off.
        Taking hold of the gear knob pull it in an upwards direction.
        Don't try and twist it as the lever
        has a spline down the front edge that locates in the gear knob.
        <LI>
        Turn the boot inside out and you will see that it's held onto
        the bottom of the gear knob with a plastic tie. Cut this to separate
        the two.
        <LI>
        The Momo gear knob comes with 5 rubber caps to place on the top
        of the gear lever. These come in various sizes and are used to make
        sure the top of the gear lever is centered in the top of the gear knob.
        Choose the 2nd smallest one. You will have to cut a vertical groove
        in it so that it fits correctly over the spline in the gear lever.
        <LI>
        Put the boot back over the gear lever but don't re-attach it yet.
        <LI>
        Unscrew the bottom of the Momo gear knob and place the threaded
        ring on top of the gear lever above the boot.
        <LI>
        The Momo gear knob came with two sets of screws. Choose the longest
        ones and insert them into the three threaded holes in the bottom of the
        gear knob.
        <LI>
        Place the gear knob onto the gear lever and push it home until it
        seats fully.
        <LI>
        Make sure the gear knob is stright and tighten the three screws
        uniformly to ensure perfect centering of
        the gear knob. Screw on the threaded ring onto the bottom of the
        gear knob.
        <LI>
        Turn the boot inside out by pulling it up and over the gear
        knob. Place a plastic tie around the top of the boot where it meets
        the bottom of the gear knob. 
        As the gear lever diameter is smaller than the old
        FIAT gear knob it's worth taking a bit of time to make sure that
        the excess material is spread evenly around the gear lever before
        tightening the platic tie.
        <LI>
        Pull the boot back down over the gear knob and click it into
        place into the center console. That's it, job done, stand back and admire
        your work.
     </OL>

<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="wheels">TSW Evo R wheels</A></FONT></H3>
<A HREF="http://www.tswnet.com" target="_blank">
<IMG SRC="tswevo.jpg" ALIGN=right BORDER=0 WIDTH=422 HEIGHT=288 ALT="TSW Evo R Wheel"></A>

I always wanted bigger wider wheels for the Tipo but could not justify the
large price they would cost. Unfortunately the car recently
failed a rather strict MOT test
due to two of the wheels being badly buckled (previous owner had kerbed
it badly). The wheels were looking rather bashed about and it needed new tyres
anyway so I thought now was as good a time as any.
<P>
Over the years my choice of wheels changed several time. I orginally wanted TSW
Imola wheels however they are a nightmare to clean with the large number
of spokes. I wanted something that looked sharp and agressive, was easy to
clean, modern and suited the car. Most of all I wanted the wheel nuts
covered.
<P>
I thought about the Anthracite finish which
looks fantastic but it also somehow always looks dirty. There's nothing
better to set off a car than a nice big shiny set of alloy wheels but I'll
leave the chrome finish for the Yanks.
The new for 1999 TSW Evo R wheels fitted the specification perfectly. The
6 spokes go all the way to the edge of the rim making the wheel
look bigger than it actually is.
<P>
After taking on board everyones <A HREF="../help/help.html#wheels">advice</A>
I settled on 7&times16&quot; wheels with a 35mm offset. The FIAT Tipo has a 4&times;98mm PCD (Pitch
Circle Diameter) which means it uses four bolts placed in a circle 98mm in
diameter. I have always been happy with the performance and looks of the
Yokohama A510 tyres that I
have used so decided to go for the later model the A520 which looks just
as good. To (1) fit under the arches (2) fit on a 7&quot; rim and (3) to
maintain roughly the same outer diameter I opted for 205&times;45 R16 tyres.
<P>
As always it's best to phone around for prices as they vary so
much. I never cease to be amazed just how much. In May 1999 I phoned around
the following companies to get prices for a set of 4 wheels and tyres,
balanced and with valves.

<CENTER><TABLE BORDER="3" CELLPADDING="4" CELLSPACING="0">
<CAPTION><B>Wheel and Tyre Prices</B></CAPTION>

<TR><TD><B><CENTER>Company</CENTER><B></TD><TD><B><CENTER>Price</CENTER><B></TD><TD><B><CENTER>Comments</CENTER><B></TD></TR>
<TR><TD>BMTR</TD><TD>&pound;700</TD><TD>&pound;40 + VAT for delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Xtreme</TD><TD>&pound;723</TD><TD>Includes locking wheel nuts</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>The Alloy Wheel Center</TD><TD>&pound;728</TD><TD>&pound;20 + VAT for delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Norwood Tyres</TD><TD>&pound;750</TD><TD>&pound;10 for delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Power Zone</TD><TD>&pound;770</TD><TD>&pound;15 for delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Smart Car</TD><TD>&pound;775</TD><TD>Includes delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Larkspeed</TD><TD>&pound;795</TD><TD>&pound;30 for delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>South West Tyres Ltd</TD><TD>&pound;835</TD><TD>&pound;20 + VAT for delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Demon Tweeks</TD><TD>&pound;858</TD><TD>Includes delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Elite</TD><TD>&pound;890</TD><TD>Includes delivery</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>Revolution</TD><TD>&pound;893</TD><TD>Includes delivery</TD></TR>

</TD></TABLE></CENTER>

<P>
The delivery price was not important as I wanted to go and have them fitted
to make sure they fitted okay. Not surprisingly I drove to
Birmingham to
<A HREF="http://www.bmtr.co.uk" target="_blank">BMTR</A> who had the cheapest price. A dealt with a guy called
Richard who was extremely helpful. They even stayed open late on a Friday
as I got stuck in a horrendous Birmingham traffic jam.
<P>
The wheels have made a big difference to the handling of the car, far more
than I expected and all positive.
<UL>
<LI>
The 35mm offset moves the wheels outwards increasing the
tracking and makes the wheels appear to fill the arches much better. The car
certainly looks more solid from behind.
<LI>
The increase in rim width from the
standard 6&quot; to 7&quot; suites the width of the tyre much better. I found
that with 195mm width tyres on the 6&quot; rim the rubber was wider than the
rim and the wheel seemed to wobble around on the rim when going around
corners. When stationary it was possible to push the wing and almost
watch the car wobble sideways on the tyres.
With a 7&quot; rim the rubber is perfectly inline with the
rim and the car feels far more stable. 
<LI>
The tyre width increase from 195mm to 205mm certainly makes the car look
more solid and can only help with grip.
<LI>
The biggest difference I noticed was the harshness of the ride. By moving
from a 195&times;50 on a 15&quot; rim to a 16&quot; rim and 205mm tyre
width the profile had to be reduced to 45 to ensure the outer
diameter of the wheel was the same. The ride is now harsher and the car crashes
over uneven surfaces much more but it's still acceptable.
</UL>
<P>
Having fitted 16&quot; wheels with a 45 profile I can't imagine fitting
17&quot wheels with a 40 profile.
They may look great but the ride must be so harsh that the handling on
anything other than a smooth road would actually be worse. I would also be
worried of damaging the rims every time I drove over a hole.
<P>
The only downside of the wheels is that the front wheels rub the wheel
arches a little bit. Not a serious amount and only when using a
large amount of steering and the car hits a bump. This will
probably not be a problem if stiffer suspension is fitted or the car
is not lowered.
If you look at the
wheel arch
from the outside of the car the left front wheel catches between
12 O'clock and 1 O'clock and the right hand wheel catches between
11 O'clock and 12 O'clock.
Within two months the tyres had worn away some of the plastic inner
arch protection. My biggest concern is that the metal lip of the wheel
arch had became exposed. I therefore removed the plastic inner arch and
bent the metal wheel arch up inside the wing such that it formed a
U shape rather then an L shape. This was done using a peice of softwood
and slowing bending the underside of the wheel arch upwards. Try to create
a U shape rather than a V shape as it is less likely to cause the paint
to crack. Some companies
will roll the arches for you if you are not happy doing it. Others will
simply cut back the lip. Note that the inner plastic liner has locators on it
in three places that are designed to locate onto an L shaped lip, These
locators will also need to be trimmed back to allow flush fitting.
The plastic liner was refitted and there
was now enough room for the tyres to clear the arches.
There is no problem with clearance on the rear wheels and tyres.
<P>
I would not advise going wider than 7&quot; rim or 205mm tyres. There is not
a lot of room left under the cars wheel arches. Any wider and you stand a
good chance of fouling the suspension components with the inside edge
of the wheel. Use could use a smaller
offset to move the whole wheel further outwards but this would increase the
tracking (good) but would also alter the steering geometry (not good). You would also need
extremely hard suspension (not good) otherwise the wheel would rub on
the wheel arch.


<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="radar">RadioShack X&middot;K&middot;Ka Superwide Radar Dectector</A></FONT></H3>
<IMG SRC="radar.jpg" ALIGN=left BORDER=0 WIDTH=233 HEIGHT=183 ALT="Radar Detector"></A>

I brought this radar detector on a visit to America in March 1997 for $215 which
was a huge saving on the UK price. It's a clone of the BEL 845 and at the time was pretty much
state of the art. It is quite small, about 7.5cm wide at the back tapering
to 6cm at the font. It's 12cm deep and 2.5cm high.
<P>
Features include an 8 digit LCD Text array so that rather than
just a set of lights coming on when something is detected it spells it
out to you.  It has forward and rear facing laser detectors. It has
VG-2 which detects when authorities are trying to detect that you have a radar
detector (yes, that's right, a radar detector detector!) and makes itself
invisible. For use in America only it has SWS whereby it can alert you to
the presence of potential road hazards and emergency vehicles signalled by
a safety radar transmitter.
<P>
On the top of the detector are four buttons, one for power/volume, one for
city/highway mode (sensitivity), one for automute and one for
Bright/Dim/Dark (brightness of display).
<P>
<B>Are they legal?</B>
<P>
In England a court case on 27th January
ruled that a radar detector
was not being used &quot;to obtain information as to the contents,
sender or addressee of any message&quot;, something which is
illegal under the Wireless Telegraphy Act of 1949.
However don't think the Police will take kindly to finding one in your
car as it's effectively an admission that you speed since why else
would you need one.
<P>
With this in mind I've used a steath approach.
All labels on the black box have been covered over with black tape.
The detector sits out of the way on the right hand side of the
dashboard between the instrument cowel and the loudspeaker. Rather than
having it plugged into the cigarette lighter via a highly visible
cord I wired the lead directly to the cars wiring loom. A switch on the
center console under the air vents is used to remotely 
switch the detector on and off. The end of the lead pokes up
just underneath where the detector sits, via velcro, on the dashboard.
It seems to work as well,
I have been pulled over by the Police for an ID check and forgot I had
the detector installed and switched on. They did not notice it.
<P>
<B>Okay but do they work?</B>
<P>
In a word &quot;No&quot;. A complete waste of money and effort.
Let's start with laser. The beam from the laser gun is so narrow that
you are unlikely to detect anything other than a direct hit. I've
had a couple of these, yes I did not see the Police officer when the
alarm when off however he would have aready taken my speed. Gatso
cameras were designed to be undetectable to radar detectors. The radar
they emmit is very weak and aimed down at the road and away from
oncoming cars. Therefore the only chance the radar detector gets is
if it picks up any relections off the road surface or from another
vehicle that's going through the camera. There's little chance of
that happening. The radar detector
picks up signals reasonably well from cameras in the opposite direction
but you have to be almost underneath cameras on your side of the road.
This of course may be down to the position I installed the detector. Maybe
if I fitted it in the middle of the windscreen on little rubber
suckers it would work better but I'm not convinced.
To make things even worse, Laser Guns and Gatso Cameras are not the only
things it detects. It can detect with 100% accuracy the little cameras
about traffic lights and several types of transmission towers. Even driving
past a petrol station or superstore with automatic doors will trigger the
detector. Therefore you can't believe every alarm which distracts
somewhat from real alarms. I now just use it as a
toy and to annoy Eva with it's beeping!



<H3><FONT COLOR="#3366ff"><A NAME="steering_wheel">Momo Race Steering Wheel</A></FONT></H3>
<IMG SRC="race1.jpg" ALIGN=left BORDER=0 WIDTH=194 HEIGHT=194 ALT="Momo Race Steering Wheel"></A>
The 5dr Tipo comes with a smart 3 spoke Momo steering wheel as standard.
However the 3 door has a rather ugly 4 spoke leather steering wheel that looks like it
came off a bus. The horn buttons are very difficult to both find and
press. For safety reasons the
centre is rather large and soft so that if you hit your head on
it during a front impact it will be kinder to your head and face.
<P>
I decided I'd had enough of the steering wheel after 70,000 miles of
driving. I chose the Momo Race as it matches the gear knob I'd also fitted,
was good quality, Italian and felt good to hold. I brought the wheel for
&pound;118.99. Be careful as there are both 350mm and 320mm diameter
versions. 350mm is small enough for road use and is still smaller than
the originals 380mm.
<P>
The Momo steering wheel has 6 bolt holes in the center. These are used
to attach it to a particular hub that's designed specifically for each car.
The holes have a PCD of 7cm (2&frac34;&quot;) which is the same
as Italvolanti, OMP and Mountry M range steering wheels.
<P>
I ordered the Hub kit (&pound;35) from Halfords who use Mountry
(Halfords code 977298). Unfortunately the
kit did not fit the Tipo, not even close. I spoke to Mountry and they
confirmed it was indeed the correct kit. I spoke to a guy called James at a
company called &quot;Brown and Geeson&quot; on 01268 764411 who are
Momo's distributors in the UK. He said that Momo still make their own
hub kit for the Tipo and Halfords should be able to order it for me from
them. I spoke to Halfords, they ordered the Momo kit and a couple of days
later I exchanged the two.
<P>
To remove the original 4 spoke steering wheel lever off the center cap. It's
held on in each corner by a rubber leg. Best way is to grab it and pull.
The whole assembly is then held onto the steering column by a 24mm nut.
This is quite large so make sure you have a socket big enough otherwise
you will be dissapointed. You have to use a socket as there is no other
way to get to the nut. Make sure the steering lock is OFF when you undo the
nut as you don't want to put strain on the lock. Best done with two people,
one to hold the wheel still and someone to turn the socket. It's on very
tight so be careful.
<P>
Once the wheel is off you will notice something quite unusual. The steering
lock mechanism is actually part of the steering wheel not solely in the
steering column. Fit the Momo hub, you will need to put some copper grease
onto the steering column first to lubricate the lock and horn mechanism.
Although the shaft is splined the hub will only fit in one position.
<P>
Connect up the wires for the horn and attach the steering wheel to the
hub using the 6 hex bolts supplied with the steering wheel. The first thing
I noticed was at long last the steering wheel now locks perfectly
level. The original wheel would only lock at about 30 degrees which
was really irritating. However when driving the car in a straight line
the steering wheel was at an angle. I took the car to a garage, explained the
problem and they adjusted the tracking rods. This took a few attempts to
get it spot on. Most places that carry out tracking should be able to
do this for you. Always a good thing to check the tracking anyway.
<P>
<IMG SRC="race2.jpg" ALIGN=right BORDER=0 WIDTH=352 HEIGHT=264 ALT="Fitted"></A>
First impressions were that the wheel looks fantastic. It completely
changes the look of the car when your in the driving seat. A much racier
appearance. Being slightly smaller is also pleasing to the eye (sometimes smaller is
better!). The Momo hub is very smart and I think it even moves the wheel
towards you a centimeter or two. I say this as I find I have to reach
slightly for the stalk controls.
The only downside is that rather than the originals nice energy
absorbant center this has 3 bare metal spokes and a hard plastic horn button.
I've been involved in a head on accident and even wearing a seatbelt my head
hit the steering wheel. The collision on the drivers side was not
so bad that the steering wheel had moved towards the driver it's just
the sheer force throwing you forward. The steering wheel was quite big and made
of plastic. Even so it split my forehead and nose open, requiring many
stiches and leaving permanent scares. The thought of hitting my head on
the Momo is not a pleasent one.
<P>
As I said earlier, the 5 door car has as standard a Momo steering wheel. It's
fitted onto a hub with the correct PCD, however it's
only got four holes not the normal 6. If
you want to fit a new steering wheel to this car then you must drill and
tap the two missing holes in the standard hub. To do this attach the new
steering wheel using the 4 bolts and use this as a guide to determine
the location that the other two holes should be.
<P>
If you want you can also take the steering wheel and hub off a 5dr Tipo and
fit it on a 3dr Tipo.


</FONT>
</BODY> 
</HTML>
